--- !u!114 &780905655
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 780905653}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 3d03c23fed709a34ba1302cc62ea4bb6, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::DescriptionPanel
  hideOnStart: 1
  descriptionText: {fileID: 658979016}
  menuPanel: {fileID: 874108913}
  pages:
  - "\tThe game is played on a hexagonal grid. The player can use a mouse, the QWEASD
    keys, or the left joystick of a controller in order to move around on this grid,
    and the camera will follow the player. The player is restricted from going beyond
    the bounds of the game, but ultimately most of the playing is up to them. There
    are only a few scene transitions, and the player can go to most places from the
    beginning. The setting of the game is an island on which the player is stranded,
    separated from the rest of the world. The player can interact with NPCs by clicking
    on them, pressing the \u201CF\u201D key while facing them, or pressing the East
    button on their controller. The player can open the menu by pressing the South
    button on their controller or by pressing the \u201CESC\u201D button. These menus
    can be explored by clicking on different buttons or selecting items using the
    left joystick and using the East button."
  - "\tThe game\u2019s primary mechanics revolve around talking to NPCs and moving
    items around the island. Through these two mechanics, a variety of ending conditions
    can be reached. The game takes place in real time, meaning that NPCs will move
    and continue to accomplish their own objectives. The game takes around five minutes
    to reach some sort of conclusion, though it can take somewhat longer than this
    to explore different endings and to master the mechanics of the game. There are
    three different endings to the game, which range from doing nothing substantial,
    helping them to a degree, and accomplishing the full extent of what you can do
    to help the people of the island."
  - "\tThere are four people on the island, including the player character. The game
    is meant to be considered as though there is a fifth character, the designer,
    who has created the path to the \u2018good\u2019 ending of the game. The player
    character, Abram, is a vessel which the player can use to affect the results
    of the other characters. The other characters include Hawa, Joseph, and Rebecca,
    each of which will be explored further below. Each has a biblical name that reflects
    aspects of the Old Testament explored within the text."
  - "\tThe overall theme of the game is a brief exploration heilsgeschichte of the
    Old Testament\u2014that God has looked over humanity for all time and that He
    will continue to do so, and that Israel is His servant in doing so. In this game,
    Abram (unsurprisingly) represents Abraham, who receives both a blessing from
    God and a responsibility to bless the others around him. Each of the characters
    will require Abram\u2019s help in accomplishing their own responsibilities, and
    Abram in turn will require things from each of them. as mentioned above, God\u2019s
    role in this story is setting the pieces in motion and giving Abram the ability
    to fulfill his covenantal responsibilities. It also focuses on the concept of
    divine justice and hesed. Though the player is not forced to perform any of the
    good acts, allowing the system created to devolve into chaos will ultimately
    require divine justice and being cut off. On the other hand, performing good
    acts will create reciprocal good acts being performed for the player."
  - "\tThere are three NPCs that the player can help, each representative of a biblical
    character I found especially interesting in my readings and in class. The player
    will first meet Joseph; representative of the Joseph found in Genesis 34-50.
    He wanders along the beach shore and is unsure of what to do. He is expectant
    that the player will find some solution for him. This is a caricature of two
    traits of Joseph as discussed in class. The story of Joseph is a story of reconciliation
    (Joseph\u2019s family must be reconciled), and so, in this case, it is Joseph
    himself who must be reconciled to the will of God, hence why he starts uncertain
    and wandering. Secondly, he is entitled, expecting the player to do his bidding,
    just as Joseph is characterized as being spoiled in his narrative. He must resolve
    these two things in order to complete his story. Firstly, he must be given an
    account of both Hawa and Rebecca, which will let him know that there are other
    people on the island he should focus on helping. Once he is given a direction,
    he is able to start acting proactively. He starts to be reconciled to God, able
    to align himself with His will. He also must be given paper and ink, which will
    allow him to start writing down what\u2019s happening to them and determining
    what they can do to get off the island. He becomes a servant to the other three
    on the island, rather than being entitled. He is reconciled with them, and by
    doing good to him, good is done to everyone."
  - "\tThe second NPC is Hawa. She represents Eve and sits in one place without saying
    anything to the player character. She has paper but will not give the player
    the item. At this point, she represents one of the interesting characteristics
    of Adam and Eve before eating the fruit, that being stagnation. She is without
    identity and without purpose until she is able to make the choice of doing so.
    Rebecca is the one able to give this to her, with the player character as the
    middleman. Importantly, it should be considered to be Hawa\u2019s choice in continuing
    and accepting the identity given to her. After this point, Hawa gives the player
    the paper, representing her willingness to continue with the overall plan which
    was devised."
  - "\tThe final NPC is Rebecca, who represents the biblical character of the same
    name. One of the most striking lessons we learned in class was the roles of Mothers,
    as a figure who has to make hard decisions in order to continue the birthright.
    In this case, Rebecca is the cinch that holds the rest of the story together.
    It is what she tells and interacts with the player about that ultimately determines
    the fate of what happens on the island. One of the important things about this
    story is that it is vague about what is going on. We don\u2019t know how the
    characters got here or why Joseph\u2019s writing of what is going on is so important.
    However, Rebecca seems to have some insight into what is going on that the player
    does not have. She is the one making the decision about what the players should
    do and is the one who ultimately guides the other characters into making the
    decisions they make. Should the player need it, she also guides them to find
    ink."
  - "\tThis game reflects a lot of what I found most interesting in the Old Testament.
    Additional themes can be identified by playing the game, but this document has
    described the most important details guiding my development of the game. Overall,
    the game has taken about thirty hours to create, and I\u2019m glad to be able
    to present the finished project. If anyone is interested in the code used for
    this project, it is available on my GitHub: https://github.com/Sdsquires27/RPGTemplate/tree/OldTestamentProject"
--- !u!114 &780905656
